 ],
 "notes": "26 stations, nos. 4-29, all present and in order. Sub-headings: \"East Coast of Scotland.\" (4-7, the Shetland lights, but no Shetland heading is printed), \"Orkney.\" (8-14), \"Main Land.\" (15-29). The 'other' column is the height of the light above water in feet. Only the first row prints \"ft.\".\n\nConventions I used:\n- Fragments have leading indentation trimmed. Spacing inside the line is kept.\n- The full stop or comma ending a station name is kept in the name, as in your \"Farn, Inner,\" example. Keeper names are given without the full stop that ends the row (e.g. \"Robert Burnett\", printed \"Robert Burnett.\"). Robert Grierson (28) has no full stop in print.\n- Footnote marks after keepers' names (Anderson†, Tulloch.‡, Harp.§) are taken out of the keeper entries and noted in 'other'. The ‖ and ¶ printed after heights (Dunnet Head 346‖, St. Abb's 224¶) are left in 'other' exactly as printed.\n- Sumburgh Head (7): the brace \"}\" joins the height to two keepers over two lines. I gave the brace in 'other' (\"300 }\") rather than in marks.\n- Italic stations are Dunnet Head (the italics take in \", Caithness.\"), Noss Head and Girdleness (only the name is in italics). Underscores are dropped from the name, and italic is recorded in marks.\n\nInconsistencies to check against the scan:\n- Dunnet Head (15) and Noss Head (17) are printed with both * (returns received this year) and italics (returned last year but none this year). The two marks contradict each other. Footnote ‖ on Dunnet Head says \"We have lost a good observer here, Mr. George Maclachlan having left.\" That fits italics (no return this year). Maclachlan is a keeper named only in the footnote, not in the row.\n- No † prefix (\"none last year, returns this year\") appears in this section. The † here is only a footnote mark.\n\nFootnotes (lines 250-269):\n- † (Anderson): \"Records and Schedule dating after 22nd Nov. were filled in by Mr. John Wilson. Prior to this date they were filled in by Mr. William Anderson, who has been since transferred to St. Abb's Head.\" So Anderson becomes a St. Abb's keeper, but he isn't named in row 29.\n- ‡ (Tulloch, North Ronaldshay **): \"No schedule was returned, but one note upon Swans, in a letter...\". The ** marks a return even though no schedule came.\n- § (Harp, Hoy Sound Low): schedule returned saying nothing of importance observed in 1880.\n- ¶ (St. Abb's Head): no returns received so far; it also mentions the Great Siren fog horn there.\n\nSpellings and counties copied as printed, not corrected:\n- Spellings: \"Whalsey\" (Whalsay), \"North Ronaldshay\" (Ronaldsay), \"Neven Kerr\", \"Montroseness\" (one word), \"Inch Keith\" (two words), \"Girdleness\" (one word).\n- Counties that look wrong: \"Chanonry Point, Elgin\" (Chanonry is in Ross-shire), \"Montroseness, Aberdeen\" (Montrose is in Forfarshire) and \"Cromarty, East Cromarty\" (an unusual form).\n- No light types (L.H./L.V.) or light characters are printed in this list.",
